---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import InlineCommand from "../../components/InlineCommand";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import TerminalBlock from "../../components/TerminalBlock";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Git as a Safety Net - AI Training Guide">
  <PageShell chapterId="practical-exercise" subChapterId="git-safety-net">
    <div class="prose space-y-6">

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-stone-800 mb-4">Git: Your AI Safety Net</h2>
        <p>
          As discussed in <a href="/development/utilizing-git" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800 underline">Utilizing Git</a>, the ability to quickly navigate your codebase's history and discard unwanted changes is of utmost importance when working with AI. The AI's speed means you'll have many more iterations, and consequently, many more opportunities for things to go off track.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-stone-800 mb-4">Know When to Throw It Away</h2>
        <p>
          This is arguably the most important lesson when coding with AI. There's a natural tendency to try and "fix" the AI's mistakes with follow-up prompts, iterating within the same chat session. While this can sometimes work for minor adjustments, more often than not, it leads to a confusing, convoluted conversation that wastes more time and tokens than it saves.
        </p>
        <Callout variant="warning" title="AI Code is Cheap – Your Time Isn't">
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-900">
                AI-generated code is cheap to produce. Do not get attached to AI-generated output that isn't working. If a thread goes sideways, the fastest path to a solution is almost always to cut your losses and start fresh.
            </p>
        </Callout>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-stone-800 mt-8 mb-4">Exercise 1: Do Everything at Once</h2>
        <p>
          This exercise will demonstrate what happens when you give an AI a vague, overly broad instruction without providing proper context or breaking down the task. It will highlight the importance of discarding bad AI output rather than trying to salvage it. Try <strong>timing yourself</strong> for the two attempts to determine which approach is faster. 
        </p>

        <div class="relative border-l-4 border-teal-400 pl-6 py-2 my-10">
            <div class="absolute -left-4 top-2 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-teal-500 text-white text-sm font-bold flex items-center justify-center">1</div>
            <h3 class="mt-0 mb-2">Exercise Setup: The Ambiguous Prompt</h3>
            <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mb-4">
              Open a <strong>fresh chat session</strong> with your AI coding tool (e.g., Gemini CLI) in your project directory. We will ask it to implement another feature: the Pomodoro Timer.
            </p>
            <TerminalBlock
                title="Your First Prompt"
                lines={[
                    { type: "command", command: "Fully implement the pomodoro timer component" }
                ]}
                client:visible
            />
            <Callout variant="tip" title="Exercise Note">
                <p class="text-sm text-amber-900">
                    The purpose of this exercise is to show how ambiguous prompts lead to poor output. We ask that you don't provide any additional context or reference any requirements documents for this particular prompt.
                </p>
            </Callout>
        </div>

        <div class="relative border-l-4 border-teal-400 pl-6 py-2 my-10">
            <div class="absolute -left-4 top-2 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-teal-500 text-white text-sm font-bold flex items-center justify-center">2</div>
            <h3 class="mt-0 mb-2">Observe the Outcome</h3>
            <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mb-4">
              Let the AI run for a bit. You will likely observe one or more of the following:
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-stone-600">
                <li><strong>Broken Code:</strong> The AI may attempt to create files but introduce syntax errors or logical flaws.</li>
                <li><strong>Incomplete Implementation:</strong> It might generate only a fraction of what's needed for a functional Pomodoro Timer.</li>
                <li><strong>Messy/Unstructured Output:</strong> Files might be placed in incorrect directories, or the code might not adhere to the project's conventions.</li>
                <li><strong>Confused Iteration:</strong> If you try to correct it with follow-up prompts like "fix the errors," it might struggle to understand the original intent amid the mess it created.</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mt-4">
              Stop the process once you see clear signs of the AI struggling or producing unsatisfactory results. Do not try to fix it within the same chat session.
            </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-stone-800 mt-8 mb-4">Fix or Restart?</h2>
        <p>
          After witnessing the attempt to implement this feature in one go, the critical decision point is whether to continue trying to fix the AI's output within the current chat or to discard it and start over.
        </p>
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-stone-800 mt-4 mb-4">
            Bad Approach: Trying to Fix in the Same Chat
        </h3>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
            <li>You provide follow-up prompts like "fix the syntax error" or "put that file in the correct folder."</li>
            <li>You try to guide the AI, step-by-step, through cleaning up its own mess.</li>
            <li>You continue to work within the polluted context of the initial vague request and subsequent corrections.</li>
        </ul>

        <Callout variant="warning" title="Why This Fails">
            <p class="text-red-800 text-sm">
                As discussed in <a href="/development/context-strategies" class="underline">Context Strategies</a>, AI models have <strong>recency bias</strong> and their context windows can become <strong>compressed</strong>. Your original (vague) instruction, the AI's flawed output, and your attempts to correct it all fill the context. The AI struggles to reconcile this conflicting information, leading to more errors and a frustrating "fix loop" that rarely produces good results.
            </p>
        </Callout>

        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-stone-800 mt-8 mb-4">Exercise 2: Discard and Restart</h2>
            <p>
                This approach feels counter-intuitive at first because it means "losing" work. However, it is almost always the fastest path to a correct and high-quality implementation.
            </p>
            <div class="relative border-l-4 border-teal-400 pl-6 py-2 my-4">
                <div class="absolute -left-4 top-2 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-teal-500 text-white text-sm font-bold flex items-center justify-center">1</div>
                <h4 class="mt-0 mb-2 text-stone-800">Review What Went Wrong</h4>
                <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mb-4">
                    Before starting over, take 30 seconds to note what went wrong in the previous session. Not for the AI chat, but just for yourself. This note becomes the missing context for your next prompt.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="relative border-l-4 border-teal-400 pl-6 py-2 my-4">
                <div class="absolute -left-4 top-2 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-teal-500 text-white text-sm font-bold flex items-center justify-center">2</div>
                <h4 class="mt-0 mb-2 text-stone-800">Discard All Changes</h4>
                <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mb-4">
                    Use Git to completely revert your codebase to its last clean state.
                </p>
                <TerminalBlock
                    title="Discarding Changes with Git"
                    lines={[
                        { type: "command", command: "git stash" },
                        { type: "comment", text: "# OR" },
                        { type: "command", command: "git checkout ." }
                    ]}
                    client:visible
                />
                <Callout variant="info" title="Git Stash vs. Git Checkout .">
                    <p>
                        Use <InlineCommand command="git stash" client:visible /> if you might want to retrieve the changes later (though for this exercise, you likely won't). Use <InlineCommand command="git checkout ." client:visible /> to simply discard all unstaged changes in your working directory. Both effectively reset your files to the last committed state.
                    </p>
                </Callout>
            </div>
            <div class="relative border-l-4 border-teal-400 pl-6 py-2 my-4">
                <div class="absolute -left-4 top-2 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-teal-500 text-white text-sm font-bold flex items-center justify-center">3</div>
                <h4 class="mt-0 mb-2 text-stone-800">Start a Fresh Chat Session</h4>
                <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mb-4">
                    Close your current AI chat and open a brand new one. This ensures a clean context window, free from the previous confusing interactions. The AI effectively "forgets" the failed attempt.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="relative border-l-4 border-teal-400 pl-6 py-2 my-4">
                <div class="absolute -left-4 top-2 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-teal-500 text-white text-sm font-bold flex items-center justify-center">4</div>
                <h4 class="mt-0 mb-2 text-stone-800">Use a Better Prompt</h4>
                <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mb-4">
                    Now, knowing what <i>not</i> to do, provide a well-structured prompt with a smaller scope that includes clear requirements and references. Using the task breakdown you created for the Pomodoro Timer, you can reference specific tasks.
                </p>
                <TerminalBlock
                    title="Improved Prompt"
                    lines={[
                        { type: "command", command: "Implement Task 1 from @docs/tasks/pomodoro-timer.md" }
                    ]}
                    client:visible
                />
            </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-stone-800 mt-8 mb-4">Why This Approach Works Better</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-stone-600">
            <li>
                <strong>Faster:</strong> Discarding and restarting with a precise prompt is almost always faster than attempting to debug and course-correct a confused AI. Each round of correction adds overhead and complexity.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Overcome Resistance:</strong> Most developers initially resist throwing away work, but with AI-generated code, the cost of generating it is near zero. The real cost is your time spent trying to fix it.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Bad AI Threads Rarely Recover Well:</strong> Once an AI conversation has gone off the rails, the context is polluted. It's difficult for the AI to regain its footing, even with strong subsequent prompts. A fresh start provides clarity.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 mt-8 bg-stone-50">
            <div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-2 uppercase tracking-wider">The Feedback Loop Workflow</div>
            <ol class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed space-y-2 list-decimal pl-5 m-0">
                <li class="text-sm text-stone-600"><strong>Write</strong> a prompt with as much relevant context as you can.</li>
                <li class="text-sm text-stone-600"><strong>Read</strong> the output carefully (not just whether it works, but why).</li>
                <li class="text-sm text-stone-600"><strong>If it's close:</strong> Make targeted adjustments in the same conversation.</li>
                <li class="text-sm text-stone-600"><strong>If it's off:</strong> Note what was missing, use Git to revert, and start a new conversation with a better prompt.</li>
                <li class="text-sm text-stone-600"><strong>Commit</strong> what works, then move to the next task.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>

        <p class="mt-8">
            After completing this exercise, continue to work through the Pomodoro Timer tasks to complete the feature. 
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </PageShell>
</MainLayout>
